title: Thermal Tolerance in Three Alpine Lichens
authors: R. Almeida · K. Sato · M. Okonkwo
lang: en
---

:::abstract
We measured photosynthetic recovery in three alpine lichen species after repeated freeze–thaw
cycling. All three resumed CO₂ uptake within minutes of thaw, and recovery time scaled with thallus
water content (see [@fig:recovery]). The results suggest hydration state, not species, governs
short-term thermal tolerance.
:::

# Introduction {#sec:intro}

Alpine lichens endure repeated freeze–thaw cycles through the growing season. Prior surveys
[@hahn2019; @ito2021] catalogued species ranges but not recovery dynamics. We quantify post-thaw
recovery across three co-occurring species, building on the method of [@sec:methods].

# Methods {#sec:methods}

Thalli were cycled between −10 °C and +5 °C on a 90-minute period. Gas exchange was logged
continuously and normalised to dry mass.

:::figure{#fig:recovery}
![Recovery curves](data:image/svg+xml;base64,)

Post-thaw CO₂ uptake as a function of thallus water content. Each curve is one species.
:::

# Results {#sec:results}

Recovery time fell sharply above 40 % water content, as [@fig:recovery] shows. Differences between
species were not statistically significant.

# References

:::refs
1. Hahn, R. (2019). *Alpine cryptogams of the eastern ranges*. Mountain Press.
2. Ito, K. (2021). Freeze tolerance in foliose lichens. *Journal of Bryology* 44, 210–218.
:::
